
---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Garden Design | Silken Valley Lifestyle"
  heroTitle="Garden Design"
  heroText="The history, principles, and art of creating beautiful gardens."
  heroCompact={true}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Garden Design & Landscape Inspiration</p>
    <h1>The Art of Garden Design</h1>

    <p>
      Garden design is the art of bringing together architecture,
      landscape, plants, water, and natural surroundings to create
      beautiful and harmonious outdoor spaces.
    </p>

    <p>
      From the formal gardens of ancient civilizations to the
      romantic cottage gardens of England, garden styles have
      evolved through centuries of history, culture, and creativity.
      Each period has contributed its own distinctive approach
      to landscape design.
    </p>

    <p>
      Explore the history of gardens, discover traditional
      landscape styles, and learn the principles that help
      transform an outdoor space into a thoughtfully designed garden.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="design-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Foundations</p>
    <h2>Principles of Garden Design</h2>

    <p>
      Successful garden design begins with an understanding of
      proportion, balance, structure, colour, texture, and the
      relationship between the garden and its surroundings.
    </p>

    <div class="design-grid">
      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Balance & Symmetry</h3>
        <p>
          Create visual harmony through the thoughtful arrangement
          of plants, pathways, structures, and focal points.
          Formal gardens often use symmetry, while informal gardens
          achieve balance through more natural compositions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Proportion & Scale</h3>
        <p>
          Consider the size of the garden, buildings, trees,
          structures, and individual planting arrangements.
          Each element should feel appropriate to its surroundings.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Colour & Texture</h3>
        <p>
          Combine foliage, flowers, bark, stone, and other materials
          to create depth, contrast, seasonal interest, and visual
          continuity.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Structure & Form</h3>
        <p>
          Trees, hedges, pathways, walls, terraces, and architectural
          features establish the framework of a garden throughout
          the changing seasons.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Movement & Flow</h3>
        <p>
          Pathways, garden entrances, steps, and carefully positioned
          planting guide visitors through the landscape and connect
          different garden areas.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Focal Points</h3>
        <p>
          Statues, fountains, specimen trees, antique urns, and
          architectural features provide points of interest and
          give a garden a sense of identity.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="design-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">A Journey Through History</p>
    <h2>Historical Garden Styles</h2>

    <p>
      The gardens of ancient civilizations, royal estates,
      Renaissance villas, and historic English landscapes
      continue to influence garden design today.
    </p>

    <div class="design-grid">
      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Ancient Egyptian Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Enclosed gardens, geometric planting beds, shade trees,
          pools, and water features reflected the importance of
          order, nature, and water in the Egyptian landscape.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Greek & Hellenistic Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Classical gardens drew inspiration from nature,
          philosophy, sacred spaces, courtyards, sculpture,
          and the relationship between architecture and landscape.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Roman Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Roman gardens incorporated courtyards, peristyles,
          fountains, decorative planting, sculpture, and
          architectural spaces for relaxation and entertaining.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Italian Renaissance Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Terraces, formal parterres, clipped hedges, fountains,
          statuary, and carefully composed vistas created
          impressive landscapes around villas and estates.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Stuart Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Influenced by French formality and Dutch garden
          traditions, Stuart landscapes featured structured
          layouts, terraces, canals, fountains, and ornamental
          garden features.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Georgian Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Inspired by classical architecture and the Grand Tour,
          Georgian landscapes embraced sweeping lawns, lakes,
          shrubberies, picturesque views, and carefully composed
          pleasure grounds.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>18th Century Dutch Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Known for precision, seasonal colour, ornamental
          planting, clipped hedges, canals, and the spectacular
          use of flowering bulbs.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>20th Century English Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          A broad range of garden styles developed, from cottage
          planting and colourful borders to prairie-inspired
          landscapes, roses, ornamental grasses, and
          lower-maintenance designs.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="design-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Garden Planning</p>
    <h2>Garden Rooms & Landscape Features</h2>

    <p>
      A well-designed garden can be divided into distinct outdoor
      spaces, each with its own character, purpose, and atmosphere.
    </p>

    <div class="design-grid">
      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Formal Gardens & Parterres</h3>
        <p>
          Geometric planting beds, clipped boxwood, symmetrical
          arrangements, and decorative pathways.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Cottage Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Romantic combinations of flowering perennials, roses,
          climbing plants, herbs, and informal pathways.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Mediterranean Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Stonework, courtyards, fountains, evergreen structure,
          lavender, rosemary, and drought-conscious planting.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Water Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Reflecting pools, fountains, ponds, streams, and
          water features that introduce movement and tranquillity.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Pergolas & Garden Structures</h3>
        <p>
          Arches, pergolas, trellises, and garden pavilions
          create vertical interest and inviting outdoor spaces.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="design-card">
        <h3>Prairie & Naturalistic Gardens</h3>
        <p>
          Ornamental grasses, perennial flowers, natural forms,
          and layered planting inspired by the surrounding
          landscape.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="design-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Regional Inspiration</p>
    <h2>Designing Gardens for the Okanagan</h2>

    <p>
      Garden design in the Okanagan calls for an understanding
      of the local climate, soil conditions, seasonal temperatures,
      and water availability.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thoughtful plant selection, shade planning, evergreen
      structure, water-wise planting, and the use of natural
      materials can help create gardens that are both beautiful
      and suited to the region.
    </p>
  </section>


<section class="garden-external">
  <p class="eyebrow">Explore Further</p>
  <h2>Discover More About Garden Design</h2>
  <p>
    If you would like to explore garden design further,
    discover additional inspiration, landscape ideas,
    and garden design services at Kelowna Flower Gardens.
  </p>

  <a
    class="garden-external-link"
    href="https://www.kelownaflowergardens.com/GardenDesign"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    Visit Kelowna Flower Gardens
  </a>
</section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .design-section {
    margin: 3rem 0;
  }

  .design-section h2 {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.8rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }

  .design-section > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .design-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .design-card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.4rem;
  }

  .design-card h3 {
    color: var(--accent-dark);
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .design-card p {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .design-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .design-section h2 {
      font-size: 1.5rem;
    }
  }

.garden-external {
  margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
  padding: 2rem;
  text-align: center;
  background: var(--rose-wash);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  border-radius: 14px;
}

.garden-external h2 {
  color: var(--accent-dark);
  margin-top: .25rem;
}

.garden-external p:not(.eyebrow) {
  max-width: 700px;
  margin: 0 auto 1.5rem;
  color: var(--muted);
  line-height: 1.85;
}

.garden-external-link {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: .75rem 1.5rem;
  background: var(--accent);
  color: #ffffff;
  border-radius: 8px;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-decoration: none;
  transition: background .2s ease;
}

.garden-external-link:hover {
  background: var(--accent-dark);
}

@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .garden-external {
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
}

</style>
